---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="3. Flash the board — AquaSense">
  <p class="kicker">Step 3 of 9</p>
  <h1>Flash the board</h1>
  <p class="lede">USB-A to USB-C only. LilyGO will not power from USB-C to USB-C. Remove the microSD card first — SD uses GPIO 2, which fights the USB bootloader.</p>

  <h2>Install PlatformIO</h2>
  <p>VS Code + PlatformIO, or the CLI:</p>
  <pre><code>pip install platformio
cd firmware
pio run -e lilygo-t-a7670g -t upload
pio device monitor -e lilygo-t-a7670g</code></pre>

  <h2>Arduino IDE path</h2>
  <p>
    Board: ESP32 Dev Module, PSRAM enabled, huge app partition.
    Install <a href="https://github.com/lewisxhe/TinyGSM-fork">TinyGSM-fork (lewisxhe)</a>,
    not mainline TinyGSM. Define <code>TINY_GSM_MODEM_A7670</code> and
    <code>TINY_GSM_FORK_LIBRARY</code>. First Windows/macOS connect may need the CH9102 driver
    from the <a href="https://github.com/Xinyuan-LilyGO/LilyGo-Modem-Series">LilyGO repo</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">Do not jumper GPIO 4 (PWRKEY) or GPIO 5 (RST). On battery, firmware must hold GPIO 12 HIGH or the board resets.</div>

  <div class="checkpoint">
    <strong>Checkpoint.</strong> Serial monitor prints <code>AquaSense boot</code> and the one-sentence idea. If upload fails, take the SD card out and use a USB-A port.
  </div>
  <div class="pager">
    <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/host/`}>← Host</a>
    <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/config/`}>Next: config the SD card →</a>
  </div>
</Base>
